1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,000

2
00:00:01,100 --> 00:00:05,580
So thanks for coming by guys, we just wanted to bounce some ideas off of you about the

3
00:00:05,580 --> 00:00:06,240
wedding ceremony.

4
00:00:07,960 --> 00:00:08,620
What's the matter?

5
00:00:08,700 --> 00:00:09,060
Are you okay?

6
00:00:09,220 --> 00:00:10,460
Yeah, it's just, you know.

7
00:00:11,380 --> 00:00:12,440
Monica said weddings.

8
00:00:14,400 --> 00:00:17,240
So anyway, we thought that one of you could read something at the ceremony.

9
00:00:17,580 --> 00:00:20,860
I would love to read a poem.

10
00:00:22,900 --> 00:00:24,900
Do you think you could get through a poem?

11
00:00:26,500 --> 00:00:27,640
Yes, a short one.

12
00:00:29,100 --> 00:00:30,800
Okay, so Ross will be doing the reading.

13
00:00:32,420 --> 00:00:35,400
Okay, yeah, I guess I could do that too.

14
00:00:37,600 --> 00:00:38,040
Two?

15
00:00:39,180 --> 00:00:42,820
Yeah, I kind of have something else planned for you guys.

16
00:00:43,660 --> 00:00:45,340
Do you mind telling us what it is?

17
00:00:45,720 --> 00:00:48,800
Sorry, I'm kind of keeping this one on the QT.

18
00:00:51,840 --> 00:00:53,760
Well, whatever it is, I hope it involves winking.

19
00:00:57,100 --> 00:00:57,540
Hey!

20
00:00:57,760 --> 00:00:58,020
Hey!

21
00:00:58,540 --> 00:01:00,780
So I just talked to one of the Duel writers today.

22
00:01:01,240 --> 00:01:01,780
What is Duel?

23
00:01:02,220 --> 00:01:03,320
Days of our lives.

24
00:01:05,320 --> 00:01:08,660
Anyway, you're not going to believe it, my character is coming out of his coma!

25
00:01:08,940 --> 00:01:09,720
Oh, that's great!

26
00:01:11,060 --> 00:01:14,060
And not only that, I'm getting a new brain!

27
00:01:16,220 --> 00:01:19,980
So great things are happening at work and in your personal life.

28
00:01:21,120 --> 00:01:23,020
Wait, what do you mean you're getting a new brain?

29
00:01:23,020 --> 00:01:26,140
Well, they're killing off one of the characters on the show and when she dies,

30
00:01:26,440 --> 00:01:28,900
her brain is being transplanted into my body!

31
00:01:31,060 --> 00:01:34,120
What? A brain transplant?

32
00:01:34,680 --> 00:01:36,700
Yes, it's a highly controversial procedure.

33
00:01:38,740 --> 00:01:39,700
It's ridiculous!

34
00:01:41,520 --> 00:01:44,200
Oh, I think it's ridiculous that you haven't had sex in three and a half months.

35
00:01:49,680 --> 00:01:52,600
It's winter, there are fewer people on the street.

36
00:01:57,240 --> 00:01:58,720
Who are they killing off?

37
00:01:59,020 --> 00:02:01,400
Uh, Cecilia Monroe, she plays Jessica Lockhart.

38
00:02:01,640 --> 00:02:02,000
No!

39
00:02:03,500 --> 00:02:06,020
She's my favorite character on Duel!

40
00:02:07,920 --> 00:02:08,600
Nice.

41
00:02:10,460 --> 00:02:14,180
She is so good at throwing drinks in people's faces,

42
00:02:14,180 --> 00:02:16,220
I mean, I don't think I've ever seen her finish a beverage.

43
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:20,160
And the way she slaps people all the time, wouldn't you love to do that just once?

44
00:02:20,160 --> 00:02:20,700
Don't do it!

45
00:02:23,280 --> 00:02:25,260
Cecilia Monroe, man, what a great actress.

46
00:02:25,480 --> 00:02:27,900
Oh, tell me about it. And she's been on the show forever.

47
00:02:28,320 --> 00:02:29,880
It's going to be really hard to fill her shoes.

48
00:02:30,660 --> 00:02:32,220
Yeah, yeah, well, help me out here.

49
00:02:32,320 --> 00:02:36,400
When you come out of the brain transplant,

50
00:02:38,320 --> 00:02:40,420
you are going to be her?

51
00:02:40,420 --> 00:02:43,700
Yes, but in Drake Ramore's body.

52
00:02:47,360 --> 00:02:49,700
Why is it so hard for you to get? I thought you were a scientist.

53
00:03:11,120 --> 00:03:11,800
I'll be there for you.

54
00:03:11,800 --> 00:03:17,160
When it hasn't been your day, week, month, even now, year.

55
00:03:17,500 --> 00:03:20,960
But I'll be there for you.

56
00:03:21,180 --> 00:03:23,220
Because you're there for me too.

57
00:03:31,600 --> 00:03:34,760
Great, so that guy there, straight or gay?

58
00:03:37,600 --> 00:03:39,380
Well, I'd have to say gay.

59
00:03:40,200 --> 00:03:41,040
Yeah, why?

60
00:03:42,220 --> 00:03:44,680
Mainly because he's kissing that other guy.

61
00:03:46,040 --> 00:03:48,820
Oh, no, not that guy there. That guy right there.

62
00:03:50,160 --> 00:03:50,600
Oh.

63
00:03:50,860 --> 00:03:53,000
Oh, yeah, he's too cute to be straight.

64
00:03:55,080 --> 00:03:56,980
Oh, knockers will help us figure it out.

65
00:04:03,360 --> 00:04:04,620
All right, straight.

66
00:04:05,800 --> 00:04:06,740
And not subtle.

67
00:04:10,680 --> 00:04:11,920
Oh, he left his cell phone.

68
00:04:12,340 --> 00:04:15,000
Oh, well, we could hand it to Gunther and he'll put it in Lost and Found.

69
00:04:15,220 --> 00:04:18,080
Or we could use it to call China, see how those guys are doing.

70
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:23,420
What if he calls his own cell phone to find out who found it,

71
00:04:23,600 --> 00:04:26,260
and I answer and we start talking, and we fell in love?

72
00:04:27,020 --> 00:04:27,960
I mean, wouldn't that be amazing?

73
00:04:27,960 --> 00:04:28,860
Wouldn't that be a great story?

74
00:04:29,280 --> 00:04:31,380
Kind of like a fairy tale for the digital age.

75
00:04:31,600 --> 00:04:34,140
Yeah, that does sound great.

76
00:04:34,320 --> 00:04:35,300
I'm going to get the phone.

77
00:04:35,440 --> 00:04:38,160
What? Wait, why? Why do you get the story?

78
00:04:38,920 --> 00:04:41,600
I don't know, I haven't been out on a date in so long.

79
00:04:41,940 --> 00:04:44,240
Phoebe, you had a date three days ago.

80
00:04:44,480 --> 00:04:48,100
That wasn't a date, that was just friends getting together.

81
00:04:48,780 --> 00:04:49,580
Having sex.

82
00:04:49,720 --> 00:04:51,680
Okay, see, I get the phone.

83
00:04:52,000 --> 00:04:54,880
No, wait, no, wait, you just broke up with Tag a week ago.

84
00:04:54,880 --> 00:04:59,780
Yeah, and until now, I didn't think I would love again.

85
00:05:01,340 --> 00:05:02,080
Nice try.

86
00:05:02,180 --> 00:05:05,980
Well, hey, wait, wait, how do we fairly decide who gets the phone?

87
00:05:06,560 --> 00:05:07,200
I don't know.

88
00:05:12,980 --> 00:05:17,880
Well, um, maybe we could, uh...

89
00:05:18,560 --> 00:05:21,380
Ha-ha, too slow! Ha-ha-ha-ha-ha-ha-ha!

90
00:05:21,380 --> 00:05:24,480
Ha-ha! Too cocky!

91
00:05:29,200 --> 00:05:32,120
I'm going to keep dating him, Mother, and there's nothing you can do about it.

92
00:05:32,140 --> 00:05:33,000
Oh, yes, there is.

93
00:05:33,500 --> 00:05:36,360
What are you going to do, kill him? Like you did Charles?

94
00:05:36,740 --> 00:05:37,860
That was an accident.

95
00:05:39,760 --> 00:05:41,180
And so were you.

96
00:05:43,400 --> 00:05:44,900
Well, at least I'm not a murderer.

97
00:05:47,400 --> 00:05:48,900
Oh, my baby!

98
00:06:00,180 --> 00:06:01,840
That was a great scene.

99
00:06:02,120 --> 00:06:05,060
And that slap looked so real. How do you do that?

100
00:06:05,200 --> 00:06:06,960
Oh, just years of experience.

101
00:06:07,100 --> 00:06:08,420
Can I get some ice here?

102
00:06:10,360 --> 00:06:12,980
Well, anyway, I just wanted to say how wonderful I think you are.

103
00:06:13,760 --> 00:06:15,360
You're not the fan who's dying, are you?

104
00:06:16,500 --> 00:06:16,880
What?

105
00:06:17,280 --> 00:06:19,840
I was supposed to meet and hug a fan who's dying,

106
00:06:19,880 --> 00:06:21,240
but that's not supposed to be until later.

107
00:06:22,220 --> 00:06:24,020
No, no, I'm Joey Tribbiani.

108
00:06:24,380 --> 00:06:25,760
We did a scene together yesterday.

109
00:06:25,760 --> 00:06:26,820
I'm the guy in the coma.

110
00:06:27,120 --> 00:06:28,740
Oh, that was a real person?

111
00:06:31,980 --> 00:06:33,520
Anyway, I just wanted to say that

112
00:06:33,520 --> 00:06:35,900
since I'm getting your brain when you leave the show,

113
00:06:36,000 --> 00:06:38,080
I was wondering if there's any tips you could give me.

114
00:06:38,120 --> 00:06:39,160
I'm leaving the show?

115
00:06:39,340 --> 00:06:40,680
I don't know. Why? Did you hear something?

116
00:06:43,900 --> 00:06:45,160
Who told you that?

117
00:06:46,400 --> 00:06:47,580
One of the writers.

118
00:06:47,800 --> 00:06:49,640
Which writer? Was it bald or was it tall?

119
00:06:50,960 --> 00:06:52,140
You know what? It doesn't matter.

120
00:06:52,360 --> 00:06:54,140
Because it is not true.

121
00:06:54,140 --> 00:06:54,520
Okay.

122
00:06:55,280 --> 00:06:56,300
And if it were true?

123
00:06:57,020 --> 00:06:59,260
How dare you come to me and ask me for tips

124
00:06:59,260 --> 00:07:01,460
about a character that I've been playing for 20 years.

125
00:07:01,540 --> 00:07:02,300
I'll give you a tip.

126
00:07:07,560 --> 00:07:08,160
Miss Monroe.

127
00:07:08,340 --> 00:07:09,740
Oh, there you go.

128
00:07:22,100 --> 00:07:24,520
No, Phoebe, you cannot get the phone that way.

129
00:07:24,600 --> 00:07:25,500
That's not fair.

130
00:07:26,420 --> 00:07:27,720
Okay, look, I have an idea.

131
00:07:28,520 --> 00:07:30,520
Why don't we see what kind of numbers

132
00:07:30,520 --> 00:07:31,700
he has on his speed dial?

133
00:07:32,040 --> 00:07:33,460
And then from that, we can tell

134
00:07:33,460 --> 00:07:34,860
who has more in common with him,

135
00:07:35,060 --> 00:07:37,160
and then whoever does gets the phone.

136
00:07:37,800 --> 00:07:40,100
Or we can decide by whoever's name

137
00:07:40,100 --> 00:07:40,720
is closer to the phone,

138
00:07:45,660 --> 00:07:46,840
I don't think so.

139
00:07:47,940 --> 00:07:48,680
Fine, all right.

140
00:07:48,780 --> 00:07:50,200
But I bet you'd be singing a different tune

141
00:07:50,200 --> 00:07:51,540
if we were fighting over a ratchet.

142
00:07:52,880 --> 00:07:53,380
All right.

143
00:07:55,120 --> 00:07:57,740
First name on the speed dial is Mom.

144
00:07:58,160 --> 00:08:00,800
Oh, I lost my mom to suicide.

145
00:08:02,240 --> 00:08:02,980
Okay, no way.

146
00:08:03,020 --> 00:08:04,660
You cannot use that to get the cute guy

147
00:08:04,660 --> 00:08:05,880
and the last blueberry muffin.

148
00:08:06,360 --> 00:08:08,160
Did I use that already today? I'm sorry.

149
00:08:08,320 --> 00:08:08,500
Okay.

150
00:08:09,480 --> 00:08:11,520
Well, now, see, this isn't telling us

151
00:08:11,520 --> 00:08:12,060
anything.

152
00:08:13,880 --> 00:08:14,680
Joe, Carlos,

153
00:08:15,920 --> 00:08:16,720
yoga, Ben...

154
00:08:16,720 --> 00:08:18,780
Wait, yoga, I do yoga. That's one for me.

155
00:08:19,140 --> 00:08:21,060
Oh, all right. Oh, so close.

156
00:08:21,220 --> 00:08:21,940
If only it said yogurt.

157
00:08:24,260 --> 00:08:25,340
Ooh, Peter Luger.

158
00:08:25,580 --> 00:08:26,580
That's a steakhouse.

159
00:08:27,320 --> 00:08:28,780
Okay, fine, meat-eater, fine.

160
00:08:28,860 --> 00:08:29,540
That's one for you.

161
00:08:31,920 --> 00:08:33,120
Oh, I win.

162
00:08:33,480 --> 00:08:34,940
He's got Barney's on his speed dial.

163
00:08:35,220 --> 00:08:37,540
So you don't know that it's Barney's the store.

164
00:08:37,680 --> 00:08:39,640
That could be, you know, his friend's house

165
00:08:39,640 --> 00:08:40,600
or a bar.

166
00:08:41,020 --> 00:08:43,500
Who has Barney's the store on their speed dial?

167
00:08:43,560 --> 00:08:44,780
His new girlfriend.

168
00:09:03,400 --> 00:09:04,840
What is that?

169
00:09:05,180 --> 00:09:07,240
I think it's the dying cat parade.

170
00:09:10,040 --> 00:09:11,800
Sounds like it's coming from across the street.

171
00:09:13,340 --> 00:09:14,840
Oh, my God.

172
00:09:15,940 --> 00:09:16,280
What?

173
00:09:16,620 --> 00:09:18,600
You know the thing that Ross was going to do at our wedding?

174
00:09:19,360 --> 00:09:21,460
He was hanging out with me yesterday and he turned to me and he said,

175
00:09:21,820 --> 00:09:23,000
You're half Scottish, right?

176
00:09:24,420 --> 00:09:24,760
No!

177
00:09:24,760 --> 00:09:25,240
No!

178
00:09:29,940 --> 00:09:38,840
No, there is no way

179
00:09:38,840 --> 00:09:40,780
it cannot be Ross.

180
00:09:50,760 --> 00:09:51,240
Unbelievable.

181
00:09:52,140 --> 00:09:53,700
Why is your family Scottish?

182
00:09:55,480 --> 00:09:57,340
Why is your family Ross?

183
00:09:59,580 --> 00:10:00,920
He cannot play our wedding.

184
00:10:01,060 --> 00:10:02,420
I mean, everyone will leave.

185
00:10:02,720 --> 00:10:04,720
It'll be like when he rapped at my bat mitzvah.

186
00:10:06,160 --> 00:10:07,920
Look, we can't just tell him no, okay?

187
00:10:08,000 --> 00:10:09,380
He's probably been practicing forever.

188
00:10:09,540 --> 00:10:10,220
He'll be crushed.

189
00:10:10,700 --> 00:10:13,080
Well, maybe there's some way we can tell him without hurting his feelings.

190
00:10:13,920 --> 00:10:14,680
I mean, come on.

191
00:10:14,700 --> 00:10:15,740
That is just noise.

192
00:10:17,160 --> 00:10:18,560
It's not even a song.

193
00:10:19,940 --> 00:10:21,320
Listen very carefully.

194
00:10:21,460 --> 00:10:22,080
I think

195
00:10:23,060 --> 00:10:25,340
it's celebration by calling the gang.

196
00:10:39,120 --> 00:10:40,220
Hi, Pheebs.

197
00:10:40,340 --> 00:10:40,860
How are you?

198
00:10:42,480 --> 00:10:44,540
Remember when we were in the coffee house

199
00:10:44,540 --> 00:10:47,420
we decided that I was going to keep the cute guy's cell phone?

200
00:10:47,420 --> 00:10:47,860
Yeah.

201
00:10:48,060 --> 00:10:50,360
And remember how I said I was going to keep it in my purse

202
00:10:50,360 --> 00:10:52,380
so that if it rang, I could just pick it up?

203
00:10:52,480 --> 00:10:52,780
Yeah.

204
00:10:53,020 --> 00:10:55,820
And then do you remember going into my purse

205
00:10:55,820 --> 00:10:57,260
and stealing the phone?

206
00:10:59,140 --> 00:11:00,200
Now you lost me.

207
00:11:00,880 --> 00:11:02,240
You stole the phone.

208
00:11:02,360 --> 00:11:03,260
No, I didn't.

209
00:11:03,340 --> 00:11:05,240
No? So you're saying that if I called it,

210
00:11:05,580 --> 00:11:06,320
it wouldn't ring?

211
00:11:06,520 --> 00:11:06,860
Nope.

212
00:11:07,440 --> 00:11:09,960
Okay, but while you dial, let me show you the features

213
00:11:09,960 --> 00:11:11,600
of my new ringing handbag.

214
00:11:11,600 --> 00:11:12,060
Hmm.

215
00:11:14,040 --> 00:11:15,320
Oh, it does work.

216
00:11:17,580 --> 00:11:19,680
Phoebe, that is a different phone.

217
00:11:19,820 --> 00:11:20,740
Oh, is it?

218
00:11:21,380 --> 00:11:22,060
Uh, hello?

219
00:11:22,780 --> 00:11:24,440
Yes, hi. Is Rachel there?

220
00:11:24,820 --> 00:11:26,520
Yes, she is. Just one moment, please.

221
00:11:26,620 --> 00:11:27,460
It's for me.

222
00:11:29,980 --> 00:11:31,500
That is damning evidence.

223
00:11:33,520 --> 00:11:34,560
Oh, my God.

224
00:11:35,560 --> 00:11:36,300
I bet that's him.

225
00:11:37,380 --> 00:11:39,200
My digital fairy tale is about to begin.

226
00:11:41,600 --> 00:11:42,220
I wonder how I should be.

227
00:11:42,320 --> 00:11:43,760
Should I be, uh, hello?

228
00:11:44,500 --> 00:11:45,740
Or should I be hi?

229
00:11:46,840 --> 00:11:48,840
Would you stop doing that?

230
00:11:49,820 --> 00:11:50,260
Hello?

231
00:11:51,200 --> 00:11:52,880
Yes, I'm the one who found your phone.

232
00:11:53,120 --> 00:11:55,340
Phoebe, you can't shut me on a call.

233
00:11:55,880 --> 00:11:56,080
Hello?

234
00:11:57,300 --> 00:11:59,260
Um, well, yeah, I mean, you could pick it up

235
00:11:59,260 --> 00:12:01,400
tonight, say, 8.30

236
00:12:02,300 --> 00:12:03,600
at my apartment.

237
00:12:03,800 --> 00:12:04,420
It's, um,

238
00:12:05,020 --> 00:12:07,760
it's 5 Morton Street, apartment 14.

239
00:12:08,680 --> 00:12:09,740
And then maybe, you know,

240
00:12:09,740 --> 00:12:11,760
after we could grab a bite to eat or whatever.

241
00:12:13,220 --> 00:12:13,620
Okay.

242
00:12:13,780 --> 00:12:15,420
Okay, I'll see you then. Bye.

243
00:12:17,800 --> 00:12:20,140
You do know that I will be here when he comes over.

244
00:12:20,360 --> 00:12:21,640
Oh, and how will you know

245
00:12:21,640 --> 00:12:22,780
what time to come over?

246
00:12:23,220 --> 00:12:24,720
You just said it.

247
00:12:28,580 --> 00:12:28,980
Oh.

248
00:12:30,540 --> 00:12:31,840
Okay, well, I was

249
00:12:31,840 --> 00:12:33,620
kind of hoping that I would just

250
00:12:34,380 --> 00:12:35,780
be alone, you know, to

251
00:12:36,620 --> 00:12:37,740
think about my mom

252
00:12:37,740 --> 00:12:39,340
and her suicide. Oh, Phoebe!

253
00:12:39,620 --> 00:12:41,340
What? That's the first time today. Oh!

254
00:12:47,940 --> 00:12:49,560
So, you like the nachos, huh?

255
00:12:50,040 --> 00:12:51,640
Myself, I'm partial. I'm 16.

256
00:12:51,940 --> 00:12:52,740
See you in 2003.

257
00:12:57,300 --> 00:12:59,560
You're absolutely right. They are writing me out

258
00:12:59,560 --> 00:13:01,800
of the show. They don't know exactly when

259
00:13:01,800 --> 00:13:03,680
it's going to happen, but apparently it's going to be very

260
00:13:03,680 --> 00:13:05,340
soon, and then that's it.

261
00:13:05,340 --> 00:13:06,760
I'm so sorry.

262
00:13:07,040 --> 00:13:09,240
Look, if it was up to me, you would never leave the show.

263
00:13:09,420 --> 00:13:11,140
Yeah, thanks. No, I mean it.

264
00:13:11,560 --> 00:13:13,420
I can't believe they would do this to you

265
00:13:13,420 --> 00:13:14,660
and to your fans.

266
00:13:15,160 --> 00:13:17,380
I mean, they're going to be devastated, heartbroken.

267
00:13:17,420 --> 00:13:18,620
They love you so much.

268
